6	/**
7	 * REAL DEFECT (2026-07-27 money-core audit, found by reading lib/fulfil-checkout.ts
8	 * against lib/stripe.ts).
9	 *
10	 * `fulfilCheckoutSession` decided what a paid Checkout session was FOR by asking
11	 * three helpers in turn — `getCheckoutOrderIntent`, `getCheckoutResizeIntent`,
12	 * `getCheckoutDiskExpandIntent`. Each one did its OWN `sessions.retrieve` inside a
13	 * `try { … } catch { return null }`. So `null` meant two completely different
14	 * things that the caller could not tell apart:
15	 *
16	 *     "this session carries no order metadata"      (a genuine wallet top-up)
17	 *     "I could not read this session from Stripe"   (a 5xx, a rate limit, a socket)
18	 *
19	 * Three nulls in a row fell through to the final line — `{ kind: "topup",
20	 * fulfilled: true }`. A transient Stripe error on a real $249 VPS order therefore
21	 * produced: card captured, NO server, NO `FulfilledSession` row (the claim is only
22	 * taken inside a branch), NO notification, and a redirect to
23	 * `/dashboard/billing?paid=1`. The webhook returns 200 whatever happens, so Stripe
24	 * never retried it. Silent, and invisible from every surface we own — which is the
25	 * exact failure lib/fulfil-checkout.ts was written to end, arriving one layer up.
26	 *
27	 * The same `null` also swallowed a metadata payload that parses but is missing
28	 * `chargeCents`: `applyResize` does `opts.chargeCents ?? quote.prorateCents`, so a
29	 * half-valid resize payload would have been silently RE-QUOTED at confirm time —
30	 * the H2 re-pricing incident, from the other direction.
31	 *
32	 * The routing decision is now ONE pure function over ONE retrieved session, and
33	 * "unreadable" and "unpaid" are answers rather than absences. These tests pin every
34	 * branch of it, plus source tripwires that the caller cannot go back to swallowing.
35	 */
